Answer: A class named Sample can be created inside package P1.

```java
package P1;
public class Sample {
int A = 10;
int B = 20;
int C = 30;
int D = 40;
public void display1() {
System.out.println(A);
System.out.println(B);
System.out.println(C);
System.out.println(D);
}
}
```

The class contains four variables: A, B, C, and D.

Answer: An object of the class can be created and its accessible members can be accessed using the reference variable.

```java
Sample s = new Sample();
System.out.println(s.A);
System.out.println(s.B);
System.out.println(s.C);
System.out.println(s.D);
```

The dot operator is used to access the members through the object reference.

Answer: Inheritance can be used.

```java
class Sample2 extends Sample {
public void display2() {
System.out.println(A);
System.out.println(B);
System.out.println(C);
System.out.println(D);
}
}
```

When Sample2 extends Sample, the accessible members of Sample can be directly accessed from Sample2.

Answer: The class can be imported and then extended.

```java
import P1.Sample;
class Sample3 extends Sample {
public void display3() {
System.out.println(A);
}
}
```

The members accessible according to the access rules can be used by the subclass.

Answer: The variables can be declared as follows:

```java
public int A = 10;
protected int B = 20;
int C = 30;
private int D = 40;
```

Here: A is public. B is protected. C has default access. D is private.

Answer: A parent class can contain a method:

```java
class Demo {
public void display1() {
System.out.println("Hello");
}
}
```

The child class can override it:

```java
class Demo1 extends Demo {
public void display1() {
System.out.println("Hello World");
}
}
```

The child class provides a different implementation of the same method.

Answer: Yes. For example:

```java
class Demo {
public void display() {
System.out.println("Hello");
}
}
class Demo1 extends Demo {
public void display() {
System.out.println("Hello World");
}
}
```

Both methods have public visibility.

Answer: Yes. For example, a method with protected visibility in the parent can be overridden with public visibility in the child.

```java
class Demo {
protected void display() {
System.out.println("Hello");
}
}
class Demo1 extends Demo {
public void display() {
System.out.println("Hello World");
}
}
```

The visibility is increased from protected to public.

Answer: The compiler reports an error because reducing the visibility of an inherited method is not allowed. For example:

```java
class Demo {
public void display() {
System.out.println("Hello");
}
}
class Demo1 extends Demo {
protected void display() {
System.out.println("Hello World");
}
}
```

The child method attempts to reduce visibility from public to protected, which is not permitted.

